  MIT; LICENSE.
  Copyright (c) 2019 Tokio Contributors

Package availability does not authorize running credential helpers, prompts
or executables. Production Git connections explicitly disable helper fallback.

The native TLS path reuses native-tls 0.2.18 already in the lockfile; it is
not an eleventh added package. Its installed source selects Secure Transport
through security-framework on Apple platforms, SChannel through schannel on
Windows, and OpenSSL through openssl/openssl-sys on other supported targets.
These are source-level backend selections, not observed new platform builds,
an OpenSSL library-version or vendoring claim, or proof of OS trust handling.
Rust package license declarations do not replace native component terms.
The local pipe fixtures do not exercise TLS, and no shipped installer or
cross-platform native-component inventory is certified by this addition.

Push HTTPS feature and native attribution delta
-----------------------------------------------

#600 retains git2 0.21.0, libgit2-sys 0.18.8+1.9.7 and gix 0.90.0. The
https feature activates git2's credential/URL support and platform-specific
OpenSSL dependencies; vendored-libgit2 selects the existing bundled native
version. Feature unification also affects checkout and the app's cade-github
dependency graph. It does not enable vendored-openssl or authorize an SDK,
installer, signing or release change.

Cargo.lock adds openssl-probe 0.1.6 alongside retained openssl-probe 0.2.1;
this is an additional feature dependency resolution, not an upgrade of the
existing version. The 0.1.6 registry checksum is
d05e27ee213611ffe7d6348b942e8f942b37114c00cc03cec254295a4a17852e.
Its complete installed source/license notice was unavailable in the two
local registry locations checked during the October 27 documentation review.
That package's source-notice verification remains a distribution-review
requirement; no notice or copyright is inferred from another version.

The existing openssl-sys 0.9.117 is additionally referenced by git2 and
libgit2-sys. Its installed Cargo.toml declares MIT; LICENSE-MIT states:
Copyright (c) 2014 Alex Crichton
This binding's license is not the license of the linked native OpenSSL
library. Native OpenSSL version, linkage and its applicable license/notices
require the actual target build/distribution inventory; no OpenSSL vendoring
or library version is certified here.

The inspected libgit2-sys build.rs selects Secure Transport for Apple HTTPS,
OpenSSL for other non-Windows HTTPS targets, and WinHTTP for Windows. These
are libgit2 source-level selections, distinct from gix/native-tls's SChannel
path noted above. CADE's push factory fails closed on Windows and other
unsupported platforms before network I/O; compilation of a native backend
does not mean secure push is enabled or accepted there.

Batch16's Darwin compiler/native receipt binds HTTPS/vendor features and
static libgit2 archives to source tree
19a6cef843bf67479c3674f9fa3728c9a7ea31d9. Its recorded libgit2 link output
names iconv, Security and CoreFoundation; the system-zlib observation above
belongs to the earlier checkout build, not a new per-target zlib inventory.
Neither package feature names nor that
local link receipt establishes native TLS/trust/proxy/redirect behavior,
Windows/Linux acceptance or the components of a shipped installer.
An owner-controlled LIBGIT2_NO_VENDOR override can defeat the requested
bundle; delivery must verify provenance rather than silently accept it.
Existing upstream COPYING/AUTHORS and bundled-component notices remain
applicable. This bounded source/receipt review is not exhaustive distribution
clearance, and missing package notices must be resolved before claiming it.

